Title = {From teacher perceptions to institutional diagnosis: A hybrid
   algorithmic-interpretive framework about innovation processes in school
   networks},
Journal = {STUDIES IN EDUCATIONAL EVALUATION},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {90},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {Teacher perceptions provide valuable evidence about how educational
   change is experienced, but they are more commonly analyzed as individual
   or contextual outcomes than transformed into reproducible evidence for
   multilevel institutional diagnosis. This study develops and evaluates a
   hybrid algorithmic-interpretive framework designed to address this gap
   in a school network undergoing a shared pedagogical transition. The
   analysis used a clean sample of 2150 teachers from 87 schools and
   combined reliability analysis, exploratory and confirmatory factor
   analysis, factor-specific composite scoring, internally normed
   classification, multilevel aggregation, school typology detection, and
   sensitivity analysis. The final measurement model retained 37 items
   across 10 dimensions. Under the principal 20/80 scenario, 27.61\% of
   respondent-by-factor classifications represented relative acceptance,
   47.37\% neutrality, and 25.02\% reactivity. Stage 2 showed greater
   reactivity than Stage 1, although this difference varied across factors
   and schools. At the institutional level, 16 schools were classified as
   Consolidated, 56 as Functional, and 15 as Tensioned. Sensitivity
   analysis identified Mediating Style, Mastery, Need Coverage, and
   Pedagogical Leadership as robust critical dimensions across alternative
   percentile specifications. The framework offers a transparent and
   auditable procedure for converting validated teacherperception data into
   differentiated institutional profiles that can support professional
   interpretation, strategic decision-making, and repeated monitoring of
   pedagogical change.},
