Abstract = {L-amino acid transporters (LATs) play key roles in human physiology and
   are implicated in several human pathologies. LATs are asymmetric amino
   acid exchangers where the low apparent affinity cytoplasmic side
   controls the exchange of substrates with high apparent affinity on the
   extracellular side. Here, we report the crystal structures of an LAT,
   the bacterial alanine-serine-cysteine exchanger (BasC), in a
   non-occluded inward-facing conformation in both apo and substrate-bound
   states. We crystallized BasC in complex with a nanobody, which blocks
   the transporter from the intracellular side, thus unveiling the
   sidedness of the substrate interaction of BasC. Two conserved residues
   in human LATs, Tyr 236 and Lys 154, are located in equivalent positions
   to the Na1 and Na2 sites of sodium-dependent APC superfamily
   transporters. Functional studies and molecular dynamics (MD)
   calculations reveal that these residues are key for the asymmetric
   substrate interaction of BasC and in the homologous human transporter
   Asc-1.},
